1
00:00:00,470 --> 00:00:01,771
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we spoke about perception.

2
00:00:02,191 --> 00:00:12,060
[SPEAKER_02]: So, say now, things that you perceive, directly, looking at me, and everything else peripherally, with the corner of your eye, even tactile, whatever, perceptions as a whole.

3
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:15,482
[SPEAKER_02]: What is a common characteristic to all of that?

4
00:00:15,863 --> 00:00:17,124
[SPEAKER_02]: What would be a common thread?

5
00:00:17,164 --> 00:00:23,209
[SPEAKER_02]: If you look at what you're feeling, like, on your legs, what you're seeing with your eyes, what would be the common thing to those?

6
00:00:24,810 --> 00:00:26,872
[SPEAKER_00]: My body being here, me being conscious.

7
00:00:27,330 --> 00:00:37,792
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, yeah, but the characteristic, the property, how would you describe, like, a certain unifying characteristic for all of it?

8
00:00:39,732 --> 00:00:46,653
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm conscious of the experience of whatever seen, heard, felt.

9
00:00:47,553 --> 00:00:49,714
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you are conscious of the experience.

10
00:00:50,154 --> 00:00:53,414
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you differentiate your consciousness from the experience you're conscious of?

11
00:00:56,075 --> 00:00:56,695
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that possible?

12
00:00:59,430 --> 00:00:59,750
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't...

13
00:00:59,770 --> 00:01:00,410
[SPEAKER_00]: I would say no.

14
00:01:00,710 --> 00:01:01,331
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly, yeah.

15
00:01:01,371 --> 00:01:02,751
[SPEAKER_02]: So, so, like, oh, I'm conscious.

16
00:01:02,871 --> 00:01:04,331
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not really accurate, isn't it?

17
00:01:04,371 --> 00:01:09,993
[SPEAKER_02]: That's... that's like a... a common attitude, but it's not really a representative attitude.

18
00:01:10,613 --> 00:01:15,735
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's... you're only conscious, or you can only even think, I am conscious of this, because it's there.

19
00:01:16,355 --> 00:01:19,256
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I'm asking you, what are the properties of things that are there?

20
00:01:20,776 --> 00:01:22,256
[SPEAKER_02]: What is... what we... how would you describe it?

21
00:01:22,317 --> 00:01:25,057
[SPEAKER_02]: In your own words, there's no right or wrong answer, as I said before.

22
00:01:26,778 --> 00:01:27,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Things...

23
00:01:28,579 --> 00:01:32,182
[SPEAKER_00]: A characteristic of them being there.

24
00:01:32,642 --> 00:01:33,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, okay.

25
00:01:33,123 --> 00:01:34,364
[SPEAKER_02]: So, let's be more specific.

26
00:01:34,384 --> 00:01:38,467
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you see his body, you see that stone, you see this cave, you see the dog.

27
00:01:39,968 --> 00:01:41,970
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you might have different attitude towards it.

28
00:01:41,990 --> 00:01:43,571
[SPEAKER_02]: You might like this, dislike that.

29
00:01:44,071 --> 00:01:45,753
[SPEAKER_02]: But I'm talking about things that you just perceive.

30
00:01:46,653 --> 00:01:47,534
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever say in them?

31
00:01:48,775 --> 00:01:51,737
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you ever say in how the... She got excited.

32
00:01:52,098 --> 00:01:55,000
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you ever say in how the cave is being perceived?

33
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:55,260
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

34
00:01:55,660 --> 00:01:57,282
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you ever say in how your eyes work?

35
00:01:58,612 --> 00:01:59,433
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the thing.

36
00:02:00,674 --> 00:02:05,658
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you have a say on how your legs are feeling the ground and whatever you're leaning on?

37
00:02:06,418 --> 00:02:08,380
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you have a say in anything tactile you're exposed to?

38
00:02:10,281 --> 00:02:12,843
[SPEAKER_02]: So, see, that's the unifying characteristic to all of it.

39
00:02:14,344 --> 00:02:15,285
[SPEAKER_02]: You're subjected to it.

40
00:02:16,746 --> 00:02:17,687
[SPEAKER_02]: You have no control over it.

41
00:02:18,848 --> 00:02:25,473
[SPEAKER_02]: Within things you're subjected to, that you perceive, on all sorts of levels, by the way, even your thoughts,

42
00:02:27,299 --> 00:02:28,799
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you have a say in how your thoughts appear?

43
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:31,300
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't.

44
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:32,080
[SPEAKER_02]: Impossible.

45
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:37,142
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it's actually even inconceivable to imagine how it would be if I were to have a say.

46
00:02:38,082 --> 00:02:38,442
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't.

47
00:02:38,522 --> 00:02:39,522
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't even conceive that.

48
00:02:40,403 --> 00:02:48,165
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, by not thinking about how impossible it is to actually entertain the notion of ownership of these things, that's how you entertain the ownership.

49
00:02:49,544 --> 00:02:53,528
[SPEAKER_01]: You can respond to thoughts, but that's always just a response to something that's already there.

50
00:02:53,548 --> 00:02:53,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

51
00:02:53,788 --> 00:02:59,514
[SPEAKER_02]: You respond to thoughts inasmuch you respond to perceptions, tactile, visual, audible, whatever.

52
00:02:59,654 --> 00:03:02,257
[SPEAKER_02]: The point is, you can only respond to what pressures you.

53
00:03:03,218 --> 00:03:07,082
[SPEAKER_02]: But, I was asking you what is the common thread to all things, and that's it.

54
00:03:07,302 --> 00:03:08,023
[SPEAKER_02]: They pressure you.

55
00:03:08,843 --> 00:03:12,387
[SPEAKER_02]: You have no say in their arising, which means you are subjected to perception.

56
00:03:13,652 --> 00:03:16,294
[SPEAKER_02]: That's when I asked you earlier on, can you stop perceiving?

57
00:03:16,655 --> 00:03:19,016
[SPEAKER_02]: You can close your eyes, but you're perceiving the inside of your eyes.

58
00:03:20,037 --> 00:03:20,878
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you stop thinking?

59
00:03:21,018 --> 00:03:25,101
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you stop having tactile sensations for as long as the body is there?

60
00:03:25,241 --> 00:03:28,164
[SPEAKER_02]: Impossible.

61
00:03:28,204 --> 00:03:41,975
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, somehow, despite all that overwhelming proof and and and and and like confirmation that you're not in control, people's minds keep maintaining the notion that you are in control of things you see, hear, smell, taste, and touch, and think.

62
00:03:43,935 --> 00:03:44,095
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

63
00:03:44,455 --> 00:03:47,958
[SPEAKER_02]: You're only in control in regard to how you choose to act.

64
00:03:49,659 --> 00:03:55,343
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, kāma and all the beings are owners of their action and responsible for the results of it.

65
00:03:56,103 --> 00:03:56,924
[SPEAKER_02]: That is your response.

66
00:03:57,004 --> 00:03:59,806
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you're not responsible for how things are subjected on you.

67
00:04:00,606 --> 00:04:03,889
[SPEAKER_02]: But how you choose to act on account of it, that's not in perception.

68
00:04:05,130 --> 00:04:06,190
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not in the pressure.

69
00:04:06,530 --> 00:04:08,712
[SPEAKER_02]: That's in you choosing to act out of that pressure.

70
00:04:10,459 --> 00:04:14,902
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why you can never ever possibly escape responsibility for your choice.

71
00:04:15,462 --> 00:04:17,283
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter the circumstances, by the way.

72
00:04:17,703 --> 00:04:20,565
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, you know, I had to kill him because he was a threat or something.

73
00:04:20,645 --> 00:04:20,785
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

74
00:04:21,666 --> 00:04:23,347
[SPEAKER_02]: You killed him because you chose to do so.

75
00:04:25,208 --> 00:04:30,311
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no such thing as I had to do this, as opposed to I didn't have to do the opposite.

76
00:04:31,182 --> 00:04:37,184
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you cannot step outside of that responsibility by adhering to, like, an external set of values and so on.

77
00:04:37,384 --> 00:04:42,186
[SPEAKER_02]: External set of values can help you gain the right perspective on your responsibility, but it's still on you.

78
00:04:42,566 --> 00:04:47,328
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why even sīla and virtue is a fetter to begin with.

79
00:04:47,348 --> 00:04:56,572
[SPEAKER_02]: The first fetter, sīlabhāta-paramāsa, fetter of virtue, sense of duty.

80
00:04:56,852 --> 00:04:59,213
[SPEAKER_00]: The fetter in the sense that

81
00:05:02,578 --> 00:05:11,605
[SPEAKER_00]: You see... you're not putting them in the right order, you're seeing them as... Well, you're not putting your responsibility first, you're not seeing that correctly.

82
00:05:11,945 --> 00:05:17,009
[SPEAKER_02]: Which, again, first you need to restrain acts of irresponsibility.

83
00:05:17,479 --> 00:05:21,001
[SPEAKER_02]: Such as sensuality, ill-will, cheating, lying, stealing and so on.

84
00:05:21,541 --> 00:05:22,762
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you need to keep the precepts.

85
00:05:23,162 --> 00:05:23,922
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't know why.

86
00:05:24,202 --> 00:05:25,283
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't see directly why.

87
00:05:25,303 --> 00:05:26,223
[SPEAKER_02]: You have some notion.

88
00:05:26,703 --> 00:05:29,765
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you do it, you are restraining your irresponsible actions.

89
00:05:30,045 --> 00:05:32,086
[SPEAKER_02]: Actions that are based on avoiding responsibility.

90
00:05:33,747 --> 00:05:36,168
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you get to discern the responsibility in regard to it.

91
00:05:38,009 --> 00:05:44,692
[SPEAKER_02]: But the point is that it all starts by recognizing that things you are subjected to, you are subjected to, you have no say in them.

92
00:05:45,052 --> 00:05:46,493
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's including your own perception.

93
00:05:52,073 --> 00:05:58,595
[SPEAKER_00]: In the suttas, they're describing, you know, like perceiving the solidity.

94
00:05:59,395 --> 00:06:02,456
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're determining through your thoughts.

95
00:06:02,576 --> 00:06:03,716
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's all given to you.

96
00:06:04,316 --> 00:06:05,236
[SPEAKER_00]: The body is there.

97
00:06:06,197 --> 00:06:07,317
[SPEAKER_00]: You're subjected to it.

98
00:06:08,657 --> 00:06:11,338
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're supposed to see how you're subjected to that perception.

99
00:06:11,358 --> 00:06:11,818
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you're not.

100
00:06:11,918 --> 00:06:13,178
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not supposed to see anything.

101
00:06:13,198 --> 00:06:16,699
[SPEAKER_00]: You're supposed to stop craving in regard to how you feel.

102
00:06:17,559 --> 00:06:17,959
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

103
00:06:18,680 --> 00:06:19,800
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if on account of that,

104
00:06:21,122 --> 00:06:35,452
[SPEAKER_02]: Towards that goal, you get to understand the nature of solidity, which... it's nothing other than basically understanding something more primordial than your sense of self, something more fundamental, something that's not... cannot be your sense of self, yet your sense of self depends upon it.

105
00:06:36,753 --> 00:06:37,333
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the point.

106
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:40,682
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like I must follow this perception and figure out that perception.

107
00:06:41,323 --> 00:06:46,207
[SPEAKER_02]: It's all for the sake of preventing you from craving towards how you feel.

108
00:06:46,727 --> 00:06:54,774
[SPEAKER_02]: You can choose to not crave, because it's going to be rooted in craving, but if you set up the order rightly, you can simply make yourself unable to crave.

109
00:06:56,655 --> 00:07:05,823
[SPEAKER_01]: And so that can be done by developing certain perceptions, because there are certain perceptions that reveal that.

110
00:07:06,680 --> 00:07:07,060
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

111
00:07:07,080 --> 00:07:14,488
[SPEAKER_02]: Certain perceptions will be resulting in the right order more accurately.

112
00:07:16,229 --> 00:07:16,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why...

113
00:07:17,050 --> 00:07:21,314
[SPEAKER_02]: But, again, unless you have a notion of what the right order is, there is no right perception.

114
00:07:21,374 --> 00:07:23,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

115
00:07:24,457 --> 00:07:30,103
[SPEAKER_00]: If you start in the wrong order, but know that there is the right order, can you...

116
00:07:31,551 --> 00:07:34,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Apply the perception to reach the right order?

117
00:07:34,532 --> 00:07:35,392
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to keep trying.

118
00:07:35,513 --> 00:07:36,273
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the point, yeah.

119
00:07:36,673 --> 00:07:45,596
[SPEAKER_02]: But, in order to do it accurately, yeah, yeah, with a degree of faith, yeah, in order to do it accurately, you must not forget that it is the wrong order you're going through.

120
00:07:46,397 --> 00:07:54,760
[SPEAKER_02]: So, don't, like, lose the context that you're actually seeking the right order recognition of it, which is not at the end of certain set of perceptions and so on.

121
00:07:55,420 --> 00:07:56,520
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I do this or I do that.

122
00:07:57,401 --> 00:08:00,522
[SPEAKER_02]: It's right there with you and you're not seeing it because it's too close to your point of view.

123
00:08:01,211 --> 00:08:03,012
[SPEAKER_02]: And you're not used to seeing things so close.

124
00:08:04,152 --> 00:08:07,253
[SPEAKER_02]: And by close, I mean right there, but not directly visible.

125
00:08:17,756 --> 00:08:19,736
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like learning to see things without looking at them.

126
00:08:20,676 --> 00:08:25,258
[SPEAKER_02]: See, I'm looking at you, but corners of my peripheral vision, I'm seeing other things.

127
00:08:25,298 --> 00:08:27,558
[SPEAKER_02]: But unusually, you don't think about things that are peripheral.

128
00:08:27,898 --> 00:08:29,199
[SPEAKER_02]: You think about what you attend to.

129
00:08:30,607 --> 00:08:38,128
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to learn how to start discerning peripheral without needing to move my face and look at the peripheral, because that's all peripheral now.

130
00:08:39,249 --> 00:08:42,749
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, that's... which was peripheral is now directly visible, actual for me.

131
00:08:42,769 --> 00:08:43,549
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, you're peripheral.

132
00:08:45,290 --> 00:08:50,330
[SPEAKER_02]: The point is understanding the nature of peripheral, not what's in peripheral, what perception is in peripheral.

133
00:08:51,531 --> 00:08:54,431
[SPEAKER_02]: You want to understand the nature of seeing things without needing to look at them.

134
00:08:54,971 --> 00:08:56,371
[SPEAKER_02]: That's like the... the...

135
00:08:56,892 --> 00:08:59,212
[SPEAKER_02]: I did refer to the internal eye, so to speak.

136
00:09:02,423 --> 00:09:09,744
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seeing... seeing, and what's implied by that seeing, that I'm seeing something...

137
00:09:09,765 --> 00:09:12,485
[SPEAKER_02]: But simultaneously, there is peripheral to seeing, yeah.

138
00:09:13,105 --> 00:09:17,226
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it is, but again, what are you seeing?

139
00:09:17,266 --> 00:09:20,347
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever you're seeing, there is peripheral to that thing that you're attending to.

140
00:09:21,647 --> 00:09:23,947
[SPEAKER_02]: That's already where your body is.

141
00:09:23,967 --> 00:09:25,128
[SPEAKER_02]: It's on the level of the peripheral.

142
00:09:25,928 --> 00:09:30,429
[SPEAKER_02]: So, understanding the framework of peripheral, without needing to look at it.

143
00:09:30,509 --> 00:09:31,949
[SPEAKER_02]: Same works for the thoughts, by the way.

144
00:09:33,632 --> 00:09:37,233
[SPEAKER_02]: There is a thought present, but then there's all this other peripheral context to it.

145
00:09:38,174 --> 00:09:44,396
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's how you get to understand, when we spoke before, like motivation, intentions behind your actions, because they're always peripheral.

146
00:09:45,156 --> 00:09:49,898
[SPEAKER_02]: The reasons why you're doing such and such thing is not in you attending to that thing visibly.

147
00:09:51,238 --> 00:09:59,621
[SPEAKER_02]: You attend to that thing visibly and actually and tactilely, because there is the intention to do so, which is peripheral to it, which is determining your set of actions.

148
00:10:02,480 --> 00:10:13,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Generally, the direction is going to be not so much that the peripheral is implied by what I attend to, as if it's kind of secondary to it.

149
00:10:13,684 --> 00:10:14,564
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually the way around.

150
00:10:14,744 --> 00:10:18,846
[SPEAKER_02]: That everything that I'm doing now is undermined by something.

151
00:10:19,506 --> 00:10:21,387
[SPEAKER_02]: That should be, hopefully, the realization.

152
00:10:22,312 --> 00:10:25,254
[SPEAKER_02]: Now it's, oh, it's because I look at this, all this is peripheral.

153
00:10:25,874 --> 00:10:32,558
[SPEAKER_02]: But then the time comes when you get used to that, you realize, no, all of this was peripheral until I looked at that, which means everything else has stayed the peripheral.

154
00:10:33,158 --> 00:10:35,619
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means peripheral is more fundamental than whatever you choose to.

155
00:10:35,819 --> 00:10:39,161
[SPEAKER_02]: And the only reason you could look at it is because it was given as an option within the peripheral.

156
00:10:39,942 --> 00:10:44,684
[SPEAKER_02]: If this thing was not there on the level of everything else that's peripheral there, I couldn't have singled it out.

157
00:10:48,466 --> 00:10:51,468
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's when you realize the peripheral is actually more fundamental than the actual.

158
00:10:54,348 --> 00:10:59,350
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can do that on the level of visual as well, if you need an aid, so to speak.

159
00:10:59,910 --> 00:11:04,191
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you're looking at the dog now, and, oh, I must think about peripheral.

160
00:11:04,692 --> 00:11:09,273
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you learn how to see peripheral, while you're looking at the dog, without stopping to think about the dog?

161
00:11:11,774 --> 00:11:17,576
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, I become glazed, I don't forget what I'm actually seeing, and I'm thinking about everything else around me.

162
00:11:17,956 --> 00:11:19,537
[SPEAKER_01]: No, now that became actual for you.

163
00:11:21,617 --> 00:11:23,038
[SPEAKER_02]: So, no, I am looking at the dog,

164
00:11:24,133 --> 00:11:27,355
[SPEAKER_02]: For whatever reason, and there is all this peripheral around you.

165
00:11:28,416 --> 00:11:30,057
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's not the point to figure it out.

166
00:11:30,077 --> 00:11:45,827
[SPEAKER_02]: The point is to learn how to be... how to not completely ignore it, the peripheral, without need... without giving to the need of attending it directly.

167
00:11:45,847 --> 00:11:49,249
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what's the overall feeling you have now, while we speak?

168
00:11:49,889 --> 00:11:51,350
[SPEAKER_02]: Pleasant, unpleasant or neutral?

169
00:11:53,657 --> 00:11:54,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Neutral.

170
00:11:54,317 --> 00:11:54,698
[SPEAKER_02]: Neutral.

171
00:11:55,098 --> 00:11:59,981
[SPEAKER_02]: That's already peripheral to any action you're doing, any situation you're involved with.

172
00:12:00,001 --> 00:12:00,801
[SPEAKER_02]: It's there, enduring.

173
00:12:01,242 --> 00:12:03,103
[SPEAKER_02]: If it weren't there, you couldn't have given me the answer.

174
00:12:03,943 --> 00:12:07,165
[SPEAKER_02]: You wouldn't even... even my question would not have been intelligible to you.

175
00:12:08,326 --> 00:12:08,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Huh?

176
00:12:09,767 --> 00:12:10,447
[SPEAKER_02]: But you understood it.

177
00:12:11,908 --> 00:12:13,028
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means that feeling is there.

178
00:12:14,129 --> 00:12:15,050
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's still there, enduring.

179
00:12:15,770 --> 00:12:16,490
[SPEAKER_02]: Time is passing.

180
00:12:16,611 --> 00:12:18,832
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the same feeling, enduring, in a course of time.

181
00:12:21,073 --> 00:12:22,474
[SPEAKER_02]: The same peripheral framework.

182
00:12:31,852 --> 00:12:35,195
[SPEAKER_00]: It's sort of like driving a car.

183
00:12:36,016 --> 00:12:41,741
[SPEAKER_00]: You need... you can't be putting your line of vision like this way, that way, like all the time.

184
00:12:41,761 --> 00:12:45,584
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to have a basic sense of space.

185
00:12:45,644 --> 00:12:49,507
[SPEAKER_02]: And you mustn't take your eyes away from the road.

186
00:12:50,128 --> 00:12:52,250
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't even afford to attend to the peripheral.

187
00:12:54,734 --> 00:12:57,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, you could crash, someone would get killed.

188
00:12:57,475 --> 00:12:58,036
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly, yeah.

189
00:12:58,476 --> 00:13:06,019
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the point is then to learn how to discern what you're feeling without needing to... because you can't look at what you're feeling, you can't perceive your feeling.

190
00:13:06,979 --> 00:13:07,980
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what are you attending to?

191
00:13:08,600 --> 00:13:10,101
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't even attend to it in that sense.

192
00:13:12,401 --> 00:13:20,645
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why the Buddha said his teaching is for the one who knows how to feel, as in, who knows how to feel, as in, knows what the feeling is in itself.

193
00:13:22,275 --> 00:13:26,237
[SPEAKER_02]: And he feels it without the need to attend to it and start to perceive it.

194
00:13:26,257 --> 00:13:27,498
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, usually, that's the idea.

195
00:13:27,858 --> 00:13:28,359
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I get this.

196
00:13:28,639 --> 00:13:31,741
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, let me look at it, to get the clarity in regard to it.

197
00:13:31,761 --> 00:13:31,941
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

198
00:13:32,201 --> 00:13:35,883
[SPEAKER_02]: That, if you go to the particular way you can attend, that's not where the clarity is.

199
00:13:36,003 --> 00:13:37,524
[SPEAKER_02]: Clarity of things, it's in its nature.

200
00:13:38,465 --> 00:13:39,645
[SPEAKER_02]: And that in itself is peripheral.

201
00:13:40,146 --> 00:13:45,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, yeah, there's a sīla where, what is that, it's the...

202
00:13:47,415 --> 00:13:50,996
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing more that needs to be done by someone who's fully understood feeling.

203
00:13:51,476 --> 00:13:51,857
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

204
00:13:52,337 --> 00:13:55,738
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the ultimate context to understand what feeling is.

205
00:13:56,958 --> 00:13:57,399
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

206
00:13:57,959 --> 00:14:10,063
[SPEAKER_02]: Thorough understanding of the peripheral context of the feeling, or simply thorough understanding of feeling, because it's always peripheral, means absolute removal for any slightest basis for craving to be there.

207
00:14:11,063 --> 00:14:12,684
[SPEAKER_02]: No craving, there is no more work for you to do.

208
00:14:14,550 --> 00:14:17,472
[SPEAKER_02]: And, by the way, it's not about some future feeling or something later on.

209
00:14:17,533 --> 00:14:19,935
[SPEAKER_02]: It's about the current feeling you have of the neutral kind.

210
00:14:21,536 --> 00:14:22,337
[SPEAKER_02]: That is the point.

211
00:14:25,019 --> 00:14:30,243
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yes, stay in the present without attending to the present, but discern it instead.

212
00:14:31,464 --> 00:14:32,785
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you can't attend to your feeling.

213
00:14:33,126 --> 00:14:34,847
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not an object there that you can look at.

214
00:14:36,913 --> 00:14:40,396
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, feeling-feels, perception-perceives, as the Buddha said.

215
00:14:40,836 --> 00:14:42,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Not perceiving-feels and feeling-perceives.

216
00:14:43,098 --> 00:14:43,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

217
00:14:43,479 --> 00:14:45,240
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be... it would be like thinking...

218
00:14:47,182 --> 00:14:51,465
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be like trying to perceive feeling, but what I'm really just perceiving is my thought.

219
00:14:51,706 --> 00:14:52,586
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

220
00:14:52,746 --> 00:14:55,128
[SPEAKER_02]: But, you have a view that you are perceiving the feeling.

221
00:14:56,029 --> 00:14:59,772
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why you'll never then find... make effort to feel what the real feeling is.

222
00:15:00,573 --> 00:15:03,475
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why you will never make the effort to remove the basis for creating.

223
00:15:04,396 --> 00:15:05,117
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll be unable to.

224
00:15:07,302 --> 00:15:13,527
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you learn how to feel, as in not try and perceive it, but realize it doesn't matter what you perceive, it cannot be felt.

225
00:15:14,087 --> 00:15:16,890
[SPEAKER_02]: Feeling can be felt, simultaneously peripheral to your perception.

226
00:15:20,072 --> 00:15:23,555
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you become much more familiar with the necessary basis for craving.

227
00:15:23,595 --> 00:15:27,879
[SPEAKER_02]: And craving, in order to be there, requires the ignorance of the necessary basis for craving.

228
00:15:29,060 --> 00:15:30,841
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, understanding feeling removes craving.

229
00:15:31,241 --> 00:15:33,383
[SPEAKER_02]: Non-understanding feeling maintains craving.

230
00:15:34,084 --> 00:15:35,805
[SPEAKER_02]: There is nothing else in between you need to do.

231
00:15:38,318 --> 00:15:42,760
[SPEAKER_02]: So, understand the peripheral, the background of your mind now, while we speak of that neutral feeling.

232
00:15:43,281 --> 00:15:46,282
[SPEAKER_02]: Understand its characteristics, its properties, its nature.

233
00:15:48,783 --> 00:15:52,645
[SPEAKER_02]: And you get to understand the right context, and you get to re-establish the right order.

234
00:15:58,689 --> 00:16:00,590
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever say that feeling being manifested?

235
00:16:00,650 --> 00:16:01,370
[SPEAKER_02]: Or you just found it?

236
00:16:02,771 --> 00:16:03,291
[SPEAKER_02]: Just found it.

237
00:16:03,691 --> 00:16:04,632
[SPEAKER_02]: But can you not feel it?

238
00:16:05,432 --> 00:16:07,033
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you not feel any pressure of it?

239
00:16:09,597 --> 00:16:10,437
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is pleasure.

240
00:16:10,937 --> 00:16:11,398
[SPEAKER_00]: It has to.

241
00:16:11,598 --> 00:16:12,078
[SPEAKER_02]: It has to.

242
00:16:12,218 --> 00:16:12,478
[SPEAKER_02]: Always.

243
00:16:13,598 --> 00:16:16,239
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you just found it, yet you're subjected to it.

244
00:16:17,220 --> 00:16:20,981
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it then right to say that that feeling is yours, in your control, obeys your will?

245
00:16:23,102 --> 00:16:25,502
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it even right to say that that feeling knows about you?

246
00:16:26,463 --> 00:16:27,603
[SPEAKER_02]: How can it even know about you?

247
00:16:29,684 --> 00:16:30,584
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't exist for it.

248
00:16:31,784 --> 00:16:35,766
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, here you are, living your whole life, revolving on account of how you feel.

249
00:16:37,406 --> 00:16:39,367
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's always been like that, and always will be like that.

250
00:16:40,306 --> 00:16:43,367
[SPEAKER_02]: Unless the mind develops itself out of it.

251
00:16:44,767 --> 00:16:47,268
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, that feeling doesn't even know who you are.

252
00:16:48,448 --> 00:16:49,468
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't exist for it, literally.

253
00:16:50,709 --> 00:16:51,749
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot exist for it.

254
00:16:52,489 --> 00:16:55,210
[SPEAKER_02]: In the same sense, upādāna cannot exist for the five aggregates.

255
00:16:55,950 --> 00:16:58,190
[SPEAKER_02]: Not knowing that, upādāna is there in regard to that.

256
00:16:59,631 --> 00:17:00,331
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said.

257
00:17:03,452 --> 00:17:06,572
[SPEAKER_02]: So, understand that indifference of whatever feeling is there.

258
00:17:06,593 --> 00:17:08,453
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter if it's pleasant, unpleasant or neutral.

259
00:17:11,157 --> 00:17:26,906
[SPEAKER_02]: Indifference to you, to your life, and then you will also understand what it means, or rather what it was before, when you were subjected to it, and when you needed to act out of that pressure.

260
00:17:27,946 --> 00:17:30,087
[SPEAKER_02]: Pressure for pleasure, pressure for getting rid of pain.

261
00:17:30,948 --> 00:17:40,393
[SPEAKER_02]: It's only done so, you only act out of it, because you still kind of don't understand that that pain and pleasure are indifferent, and your actions are equally insignificant to it.

262
00:17:41,191 --> 00:17:45,412
[SPEAKER_02]: Your actions cannot make a difference to the fact that you're subjected to feelings.

263
00:17:46,273 --> 00:17:48,353
[SPEAKER_02]: Your actions are only secondary result.

264
00:17:48,893 --> 00:17:54,875
[SPEAKER_02]: Your response, your kind of desperate response towards being subjected to feelings.

265
00:17:54,935 --> 00:18:03,678
[SPEAKER_02]: But, if your actions would make a difference to it, well, you would have already been free from being subjected to feelings, because you acted every possible way in this life.

266
00:18:04,398 --> 00:18:08,180
[SPEAKER_02]: You said yes, you said no, you said neither, you abstained, you were unclear.

267
00:18:08,320 --> 00:18:09,220
[SPEAKER_02]: All of those movements

268
00:18:10,483 --> 00:18:11,204
[SPEAKER_02]: The pressure was there.

269
00:18:11,304 --> 00:18:12,165
[SPEAKER_02]: Made no difference to it.

270
00:18:12,465 --> 00:18:14,967
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to understand that pressure doesn't care about your movements.

271
00:18:15,848 --> 00:18:16,208
[SPEAKER_02]: Cannot.

272
00:18:16,328 --> 00:18:17,269
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't even know about it.

273
00:18:18,170 --> 00:18:31,202
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then you can restrain the movements that would be perpetuating need to move, which is movement of desire, greed, aversion, anger, distraction, delusion, carelessness.

274
00:18:33,132 --> 00:18:34,193
[SPEAKER_02]: And again, that's what Buddha said.

275
00:18:34,413 --> 00:18:35,734
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't, like, stop the pressure.

276
00:18:35,754 --> 00:18:36,575
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't stop thoughts.

277
00:18:36,595 --> 00:18:37,696
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't stop the perception.

278
00:18:38,436 --> 00:18:40,798
[SPEAKER_02]: But you can stop acting unwholesomely out of it.

279
00:18:47,123 --> 00:18:51,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you understand that it's indifference.

280
00:18:51,507 --> 00:18:52,627
[SPEAKER_02]: You understand the nature of it.

281
00:18:52,647 --> 00:18:55,850
[SPEAKER_02]: You understand that you cannot partake in it.

282
00:18:56,150 --> 00:18:59,011
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot command it, you cannot will it, you cannot control it.

283
00:18:59,171 --> 00:19:01,931
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, any attempt at controlling it becomes redundant.

284
00:19:02,371 --> 00:19:08,533
[SPEAKER_02]: You're only attempting to control it, because you still have an implicit view that you can't, in some manner.

285
00:19:10,233 --> 00:19:13,774
[SPEAKER_02]: And for as long as you don't remove that view, you're not removing desire and lust and anger.

286
00:19:20,495 --> 00:19:25,596
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any attempt to control that feeling, or get rid of that feeling, becomes meaningless.

287
00:19:26,744 --> 00:19:27,104
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

288
00:19:27,244 --> 00:19:27,985
[SPEAKER_02]: Utterly meaningless.

289
00:19:29,766 --> 00:19:31,327
[SPEAKER_02]: Lust becomes utterly meaningless.

290
00:19:31,467 --> 00:19:33,448
[SPEAKER_02]: Anger, acting out of anger, becomes inconceivable.

291
00:19:34,929 --> 00:19:39,012
[SPEAKER_02]: Not because you need to go through the psychological movement of anger is bad, anger is not good.

292
00:19:39,032 --> 00:19:41,353
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's just inconceivable to do so.

293
00:19:41,373 --> 00:19:44,275
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no need for restraint.

294
00:19:44,335 --> 00:19:46,637
[SPEAKER_01]: It just doesn't make any sense anymore.

295
00:20:06,488 --> 00:20:07,069
[SPEAKER_01]: She's calmed down.

296
00:20:07,710 --> 00:20:08,010
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

297
00:20:09,332 --> 00:20:10,914
[SPEAKER_02]: She still has that wild side to her.

298
00:20:10,934 --> 00:20:12,957
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, she's been with us for three weeks on that thing.

299
00:20:14,299 --> 00:20:16,021
[SPEAKER_02]: But she's quite a good quick learner.

300
00:20:18,845 --> 00:20:19,425
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean.

301
00:20:19,526 --> 00:20:24,112
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, even when you read the suttas and all these perceptions of this and infinite space,

302
00:20:26,660 --> 00:20:28,121
[SPEAKER_02]: One step at a time.

303
00:20:28,141 --> 00:20:36,464
[SPEAKER_02]: As the Buddha said, even in terms of like jhānas, if you... oh, a mind wants to enter second jhāna, hasn't even established itself, become familiar with the first one, you'll enter neither.

304
00:20:36,964 --> 00:20:38,524
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll establish your mind upon neither.

305
00:20:40,525 --> 00:20:43,806
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's the same principle that's just being refined further and further.

306
00:20:43,826 --> 00:20:48,328
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you haven't even established the basis of that principle, how can you refine it when you have nothing to refine?

307
00:20:50,036 --> 00:20:59,721
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's always come back to either present situation of the body, body posture, whichever way it's enduring, perceptions you're subjected to, feeling that's enduring peripherally.

308
00:21:00,142 --> 00:21:00,902
[SPEAKER_02]: That is the framework.

309
00:21:01,142 --> 00:21:05,185
[SPEAKER_02]: And, by the way, you don't need to understand more than that.

310
00:21:05,565 --> 00:21:12,609
[SPEAKER_02]: Understand the body being present there on its own, indifferent, ignorant of you, doesn't even know about your existence.

311
00:21:12,789 --> 00:21:18,112
[SPEAKER_02]: Understand that to the extent necessary for removal of all the lust, aversion, delusion, and you'll be an arahant.

312
00:21:19,573 --> 00:21:27,318
[SPEAKER_02]: And no need to think about perceptions of infinite space and so on, because you already covered it.

313
00:21:46,263 --> 00:21:47,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it does speak of...

314
00:21:48,145 --> 00:21:57,776
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, in regard to that, you're saying that's just the baseline of what you need to understand to be liberated, free from suffering.

315
00:21:58,257 --> 00:22:06,546
[SPEAKER_02]: But they were arahants that had extended knowledge in regard to... Extended knowledge, but doesn't matter how extended it was, it was within the thing you understood.

316
00:22:06,706 --> 00:22:06,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

317
00:22:07,482 --> 00:22:10,324
[SPEAKER_02]: It wasn't like an extra, an addendum, or something like that.

318
00:22:10,764 --> 00:22:14,126
[SPEAKER_00]: But some people, like, sort of, conflate that, like, all arahants had jhānas.

319
00:22:14,226 --> 00:22:18,809
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, they conflate with all this diversity of knowledge, and all these different things you get to figure out.

320
00:22:20,152 --> 00:22:28,254
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it's just different aspects, more refined aspects, within the same frame, the same thing, which is suffering and freedom from craving in regard to it.

321
00:22:28,274 --> 00:22:33,555
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha was able to distinguish between different types of arahants.

322
00:22:33,675 --> 00:22:35,575
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, they're all arahants.

323
00:22:35,935 --> 00:22:36,135
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

324
00:22:36,435 --> 00:22:38,596
[SPEAKER_01]: But you got the body witness, were they arahants?

325
00:22:38,636 --> 00:22:39,376
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't remember now.

326
00:22:39,756 --> 00:22:39,976
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

327
00:22:40,016 --> 00:22:41,296
[SPEAKER_02]: Like the...

328
00:22:41,316 --> 00:22:42,677
[SPEAKER_02]: It is all the one with wisdom?

329
00:22:42,757 --> 00:22:42,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

330
00:22:43,197 --> 00:22:43,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

331
00:22:43,897 --> 00:22:48,878
[SPEAKER_01]: The ones who were liberated both ways, they were arahants, but just had different...

332
00:22:50,088 --> 00:22:56,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether they've gone through all these establishments of mind.

